<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.2. Solving a Sturm-Liouville problem</title>

  <alert>MTH 401</alert>



  <definition xml:id="def-sturm-liouville">
    <statement>
      <p>
      A general <term>Sturm-Liouville problem</term> has the form 
      <md>
        <mrow>\ds \frac{d}{dx}\left[p(x)\frac{du}{dx}\right] + q(x)u \amp = -\lambda \omega(x)u </mrow>
        <mrow> u(a) \amp = c_1</mrow>
        <mrow> u(b) \amp = c_2</mrow>
      </md>
      where <m>a, b</m> are the extreme boundary conditions of <m>x</m>. Solving a problem means finding the 
     the <term>eigenvalues</term>, <m>\lambda</m>, that correspond to non-trivial solutions, and then find the 
      corresponding solutions or <term>eigenfunctions</term> <m>u = u(x)</m> associated to each <m>\lambda</m>.
    </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

    
  <remark>
    <p>
      We'll mostly focus on the simpler form of the problem: (not always called <m>X</m>!):
      <md>
        <mrow> X'' \amp = -kX </mrow>
        <mrow>X(a) \amp = 0 </mrow>
        <mrow>X(b) \amp = 0. </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>


  <exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>Consider the homogeneous heat equation when the ends
are insulated, i.e.
<md>
<mrow>\ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} \amp  =   6\frac{\partial^2
                                      u}{\partial^2 x^2}</mrow>
<mrow>\ds \pd{u}{x}(0, t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
<mrow>\ds \pd{u}{x}(10, t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
</md></p>
  </introduction>

    <task workspace="1.8in">
      <statement>
      <p>First apply separation of variables;  that is, 
  look for a solution with the form
  <m> u(x,t) = X(x) T(t).</m>
  This will enable us to write the partial
  derivatives of <m>u</m> in terms of simpler ordinary derivatives of <m>X</m> and <m>T</m>.
  Find <m>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}, \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial^2 x^2}</m> in terms of <m>X(x), T(t)</m>, 
  substitute into the heat equation,
  and rearrange the equation so that all occurrences of <m>x</m> are on one
  side and all the occurrences of <m>t</m> are on the other.  This leads to
  a separation constant <m>-k</m>.  You should then find (a) an ordinary
  differential equation for <m>T</m>, (b) an ordinary differential equation
  for <m>X</m>, and (c) two boundary conditions for <m>X</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </task>


  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>The differential equation and boundary conditions for <m>X</m> are
  called a <em>Sturm-Liouville</em> problem.  As in the video, look for
  eigenvalues <m>k</m> and eigenfunctions <m>X</m> by considering</p>
<task workspace="1.8in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <term>Case 1</term>:  <m>k &lt; 0</m> so that <m>k=-m^2</m>.  The differential equation
  <m>X''=m^2X</m> has solutions that are linear combinations of a growing
  and decaying exponential.
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>
<task workspace="1.8in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <term>Case 2</term>:  <m>k = 0</m> so the differential equation for <m>X</m> is
  <m>X''=0</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>
<task workspace="1.8in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <term>Case 3</term>:  <m>k &gt; 0</m> so that <m>k=m^2</m>.  The differential equation
  <m>X''=-m^2X</m> has solutions that are linear combinations of a sine and
  a cosine function.
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>
    
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Summarize your findings by stating the eigenvalues and
  eigenfunctions to this Sturm-Liouville problem:
  <md>
  \begin{aligned}
    X'' \amp  = -kX \\
    X'(0) \amp  = 0 \\
    X'(L) \amp  = 0 \\
  \end{aligned}
  </md></p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>When <m>L=10</m>, sketch the graphs of the eigenfunctions
  corresponding to the three smallest eigenvalues.</p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>For each eigenvalue, solve the differential equation for <m>T(t)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Write the general solution to the original heat equation with boundary
  conditions given on the first page.</p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
